#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115040 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115213 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "348ff9ef-8ffe-57aa-bafa-9d1a197178fc" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing a Constant on One Side"}] :question/id #uuid "d833825d-4ac4-556d-95a1-bb328d5b5bb4" :question/math-academy-id "e-4149" :question/problem "If $\\log_2(x^2-6)=\\log_2(10),$ then what is the value of $x?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2}-6) & ={log}_{2}⁡(10) \\\\ {x}^{2}-6 & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =16 \\\\ x & =\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=4$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2}-6) & ={log}_{2}⁡({4}^{2}-6) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(16-6) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(10).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=4$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=-4$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2}-6) & ={log}_{2}⁡((-4)^{2}-6) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(16-6) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(10).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-4$ is a valid solution.\n\nSo, we conclude that the solutions are $x = \\pm 4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115042 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115214 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d03f79a4-06f3-5324-a1ec-687659956384" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing Nonlinear Arguments"}] :question/id #uuid "088cff27-4c06-542c-876b-d3d2ed7b0504" :question/math-academy-id "e-4150" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\ln (2x^2)=\\ln (3x).$" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =3x \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-3x & =0 \\\\ x(2x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=0$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2(0)^{2}) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3(0)) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not valid because $\\ln \\left(0\\right)$ is undefined. Therefore, $x=0$ is not a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=\\dfrac{3}{2}$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3(\\frac{3}{2})) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{2}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{2}).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=\\dfrac{3}{2}$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $x =\\dfrac{3}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115043 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115215 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9025fa2d-5f74-5187-a225-234da9b9810b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing Linear Arguments on Both Sides"}] :question/id #uuid "d164cff6-3d50-57c8-a118-8681aaf0c1f8" :question/math-academy-id "e-4151" :question/problem "Solve $\\log (x-1)=\\log (2x+1)$ for $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(x-1) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x+1) \\\\ x-1 & =2x+1 \\\\ x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=-2$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(x-1) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x+1) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(-2-1) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(2(-2)+1) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(-3) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\log (-3)$ is not a real number.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the equation has no real solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233039 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115213 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "348ff9ef-8ffe-57aa-bafa-9d1a197178fc" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing a Constant on One Side"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "150e2764-cae5-5097-b0e0-4013ce39bd89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538277} {:answer/id #uuid "1dc865a8-a99f-55ed-b80f-d011253dcedc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538298} {:answer/id #uuid "3b88005d-c042-5ac4-a802-6c3fbb160726" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538354} {:answer/id #uuid "71fef0a9-d275-50cc-a7a9-2d08bb857707" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538486} {:answer/id #uuid "ed2c6624-a345-5138-8ba0-fd272edd299d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1dc865a8-a99f-55ed-b80f-d011253dcedc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538298} :answer-field/id #uuid "f312c34a-9866-5b7f-a9d8-bf68bba44d49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "58031be4-0ee8-501f-8ac5-6ad86baa2337" :question/math-academy-id "q-100706" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\operatorname{log}⁡(x-2)=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{log}⁡(256).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the power rule to simplify the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(x-2) & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{log}⁡(256) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(x-2) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({256}^{1/4}) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(x-2) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(x-2) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(4) \\\\ x-2 & =4 \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=6$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(x-2) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(6-2) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(4) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({256}^{1/4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{log}⁡(256).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=6$ is a valid solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546998 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115215 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9025fa2d-5f74-5187-a225-234da9b9810b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing Linear Arguments on Both Sides"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b439e4f-29b5-5860-82f4-48f94dd1db4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546308} {:answer/id #uuid "3e0de288-e17e-5bf7-bd37-46f5c2f0e4c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No real solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546389} {:answer/id #uuid "76c91ffe-1f19-5e63-972d-f33c42832ac7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546516} {:answer/id #uuid "b8c1ffbe-1de5-51fd-b0a4-5b7d3d8d008b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546665} {:answer/id #uuid "d1f644a8-4fa0-5151-8a54-b0b8fac4da61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546715}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e0de288-e17e-5bf7-bd37-46f5c2f0e4c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No real solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546389} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5df186d-949c-5c4d-8754-3946a2fd4215" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546943}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "454648d5-f41c-5a23-83bf-f9148d806a00" :question/math-academy-id "q-100763" :question/problem "Solve $\\operatorname{log}⁡(4x)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(5x+9)$ for $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(4x) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(5x+9) \\\\ 4x & =5x+9 \\\\ x & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=-9$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(4x) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(5x+9) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(4(-9)) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(5(-9)+9) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(-36) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(-36)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\operatorname{log}⁡(-36)$ is not a real number.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the equation has no real solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547065 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115215 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9025fa2d-5f74-5187-a225-234da9b9810b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing Linear Arguments on Both Sides"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c32042f-9a1b-5a1c-90ed-857badec8638" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546384} {:answer/id #uuid "56958972-94b5-571f-b4ba-1604c2f5214e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546441} {:answer/id #uuid "5bd27eab-1cb3-5722-8b8e-6550c41efc2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546451} {:answer/id #uuid "e65f4b49-cc59-56d2-ad31-186cc7327c2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No real solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546765} {:answer/id #uuid "ff575287-1487-5af4-b77a-4cb9f14cf356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546826}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff575287-1487-5af4-b77a-4cb9f14cf356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546826} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad1a629b-9044-5158-8f0e-280cd361d793" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "de567b40-8a36-58e6-967e-d32432dbd495" :question/math-academy-id "q-100769" :question/problem "Given that $\\operatorname{log}⁡(3x-5)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(7-x),$ what is the value of $x?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(3x-5) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(7-x) \\\\ 3x-5 & =7-x \\\\ 4x & =12 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=3$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(3(3)-5) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(7-3) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(4) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=3$ is a valid solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539022 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115214 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d03f79a4-06f3-5324-a1ec-687659956384" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing Nonlinear Arguments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79de5745-5bf0-555e-a601-3b494c6bd938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538509} {:answer/id #uuid "8be64e16-04ad-51d8-827e-a16e9b00296b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538560} {:answer/id #uuid "a1508db0-4863-5e06-8a60-94207ae22916" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No real solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538617} {:answer/id #uuid "add9a8e9-c820-5de7-a37b-45ae258e7857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538642} {:answer/id #uuid "b7237201-cb21-5414-84de-7f2cfcb1a828" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538660}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1508db0-4863-5e06-8a60-94207ae22916" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No real solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538617} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0a61e66-3aca-536b-aa8a-9b7a2020142b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ecdce78b-6ad1-5fcc-9999-f701ae74931a" :question/math-academy-id "q-100773" :question/problem "Solve the equation ${log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2}+7)={log}_{2}⁡(2{x}^{2}+8).$" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2}+7) & ={log}_{2}⁡(2{x}^{2}+8) \\\\ {x}^{2}+7 & =2{x}^{2}+8 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation ${x}^{2}=-1$ has no real solutions. Therefore, our original logarithmic equation has no solutions either."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547014 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115213 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "348ff9ef-8ffe-57aa-bafa-9d1a197178fc" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing a Constant on One Side"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01559a85-3d08-5f2b-b75b-e99183bdf0aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546256} {:answer/id #uuid "15629f88-a1af-5330-a437-f1ef36d4d68e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546297} {:answer/id #uuid "7efc7d17-e9cc-5d33-8433-df8a51e85e89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546533} {:answer/id #uuid "bef3d0be-49bf-5026-9467-bcf81d5d0568" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546679} {:answer/id #uuid "f49739f0-48e8-5f89-bde8-5a70ceaae178" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546802}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f49739f0-48e8-5f89-bde8-5a70ceaae178" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546802} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b216247-45f8-5924-bd09-17bdfae3c6c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6dbcbc9c-b30e-515a-9390-1cde473a0411" :question/math-academy-id "q-110796" :question/problem "If ${log}_{5}⁡(4-x)=2{log}_{5}⁡2,$ then what is the value of $x?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the power rule to simplify the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}⁡(4-x) & =2{log}_{5}⁡2 \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡(4-x) & ={log}_{5}⁡{2}^{2} \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡(4-x) & ={log}_{5}⁡4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}⁡(4-x) & ={log}_{5}⁡4 \\\\ 4-x & =4 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=0$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}⁡(4-x) & ={log}_{5}⁡(4-0) \\\\  & ={log}_{5}⁡(4) \\\\  & ={log}_{5}⁡({2}^{2}) \\\\  & =2{log}_{5}⁡2.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=0$ is a valid solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547058 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115213 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "348ff9ef-8ffe-57aa-bafa-9d1a197178fc" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing a Constant on One Side"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "293f6fac-2a04-5dc8-a0a3-e9394c0f7b0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546340} {:answer/id #uuid "5df44419-fb9b-5b67-b386-8e33fa51a0a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546453} {:answer/id #uuid "985ddd2f-5a93-55b9-b6eb-39e6e65c2266" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546589} {:answer/id #uuid "adef868d-94c1-557f-b02b-4ee72c5adbd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546641} {:answer/id #uuid "dfdfa689-1723-5c62-9474-88ab151694aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5df44419-fb9b-5b67-b386-8e33fa51a0a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546453} :answer-field/id #uuid "389631a9-06db-578a-b331-cd015ccdd6d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d3ac80cd-6459-5be8-953c-5d7b28bc8da8" :question/math-academy-id "q-79866" :question/problem "Solve the equation ${log}_{2}⁡(2x-5)=\\frac{1}{3}{log}_{2}⁡(27).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the power rule to simplify the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(2x-5) & =\\frac{1}{3}{log}_{2}⁡(27) \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(2x-5) & ={log}_{2}⁡({27}^{1/3}) \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(2x-5) & ={log}_{2}⁡(3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(2x-5) & ={log}_{2}⁡(3) \\\\ 2x-5 & =3 \\\\ 2x & =8 \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=4$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(2x-5) & ={log}_{2}⁡(2(4)-5) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{log}_{2}⁡(27).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=4$ is a valid solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546959 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115214 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d03f79a4-06f3-5324-a1ec-687659956384" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing Nonlinear Arguments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21c7f66e-df91-5a0b-81c5-8323dd0749cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546323} {:answer/id #uuid "27392c0f-6fc9-5c3d-94bd-a227478f1528" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546338} {:answer/id #uuid "64c71b5a-1c3f-56db-ac11-a90b6661d9c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546472} {:answer/id #uuid "7e9deabd-ac4b-5f55-8c56-eb4b90d311b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546531} {:answer/id #uuid "f94bcb29-f716-5ccb-8260-3a2a73f6fdb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21c7f66e-df91-5a0b-81c5-8323dd0749cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546323} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc88efbc-b8e0-5750-b595-44d875bff8cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546956}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0107835e-4e3e-5394-afc5-c0c0506cc080" :question/math-academy-id "q-79873" :question/problem "Solve the equation ${log}_{6}⁡({x}^{2}+20)={log}_{6}⁡(-10x-5).$" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{6}⁡({x}^{2}+20) & ={log}_{6}⁡(-10x-5) \\\\ {x}^{2}+20 & =-10x-5 \\\\ {x}^{2}+10x+25 & =0 \\\\ (x+5)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n- Substituting $x=-5$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{6}⁡({x}^{2}+20) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{6}⁡(-10x-5) \\\\ {log}_{6}⁡((-5)^{2}+20) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{6}⁡(-10(-5)-5) \\\\ {log}_{6}⁡(45) & ={log}_{6}⁡(45).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-5$ is a valid solution."}]}
